11
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:40,100
Key market participants.

12
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:46,100
It is obvious that there is a buyer for every seller and a seller for every buyer.

13
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:53,096
Whilst it is obvious what many fail to understand is what time frame buyer or seller is making the sale or purchase.

14
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:59,200
This powerful piece of knowledge unlocks key information about potential for future direction.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
What do we mean by this?

16
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:07,369
Well, simply put, if we were to observe prices going higher in a market, the basic assumption would be that

17
00:01:07,369 --> 00:01:10,598
there are buyers or more buyers than sellers in the market.

18
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,393
However, sometimes it's not actually buyers

19
00:01:13,393 --> 00:01:15,718
that are taking the market higher, but

20
00:01:15,718 --> 00:01:18,043
shorts that are covering their shorts and

21
00:01:18,043 --> 00:01:21,697
buying out of those shorts which is taking the market higher.

22
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:29,700
Understanding what moves prices or what time frame is moving prices or which participants is key to unlocking future direction.

23
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,778
By observing the auction process unfolding via

24
00:01:33,778 --> 00:01:39,899
volume profiling, we can begin to assess the attitude and interaction of different time frame participants.

25
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:47,835
It's imperative guys, as we go through the rest of this module and the strategies in module 2 and 3, that you understand how these different participants are interacting.

26
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:55,500
Because it's this interaction that is going to lead to success or failure of the various trade setups.

27
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:01,900
Let's talk about the three major participants. Commercial, speculative and automated.

28
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:05,900
The first and most influential, commercial.

29
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:14,900
This includes pension funds, insurance funds, hedge funds, large institutions like banks and hedges or hedging strategies.

30
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,247
Now, in order to understand how commercial

31
00:02:18,247 --> 00:02:20,894
participants move the market, we need to

32
00:02:20,894 --> 00:02:24,298
first look at the characteristics that define commercial participants.

33
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:29,300
The first characteristic, not price sensitive. What does this mean?

34
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,900
If we would take a basic example of a hedge fund.

35
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,500
Let's say they get into a long position and the price starts to go against them.

36
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:46,285
They're not too concerned about which price they get out at.Why? Because their objective is to just lock in profits.

37
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:51,700
So once they get out, they are executing at the best price possible regardless of where it is.

38
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:57,900
Equally, if we take the example of a pension fund, every month they need to rebalance their portfolios.

39
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:04,298
If at the end of the month they find that themselves short of bonds, they have to buy those bonds due to regulation.

40
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:12,596
So they are not going to be calling up the banks and worrying too much about price, they are merely going to execute at the best price possible.

41
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,818
This lack of being price sensitive can sometimes drive markets beyond certain

42
00:03:16,818 --> 00:03:19,096
points that we are used to and it

43
00:03:19,096 --> 00:03:22,799
is important to understand why they are being driven in such a way.

44
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:31,100
Our next characteristic, natural hedging. A lot of the large institutions tend to execute via hedging strategy.

45
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:34,800
This means they are long one market and short another.

46
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:41,024
This can sometimes create confusion as we would expect all bond markets to be highly correlated,

47
00:03:41,024 --> 00:03:43,397
yet sometimes they move in opposite directions.

48
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:49,200
It's important to understand these hedges as this can cause us a lot of frustration in various setups.

49
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,700
The next characteristic, capital preservation.

50
00:03:53,200 --> 00:04:01,100
Again, a crucial understanding for commercial paper is that they are expected to preserve capital at all costs.

51
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:08,468
And that is why sometimes we can see some very aggressive moves in one direction and the other as these

52
00:04:08,468 --> 00:04:12,998
large institutions enter into the market with paper in order to preserve capital.

53
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:15,300
Trade value.

54
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:20,200
Large commercial institutions don't always participate in the market.

55
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:24,300
They rarely participate within the middle of ranges.

56
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:29,500
They tend to be found at the lows and highs of certain moves in markets.

57
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:30,200
Why?

58
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:34,320
Because their objective is to buy when a market is perceived cheap

59
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:40,200
or at a discount and sell when a market is at a premium or at a high price.

60
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:43,700
It's important to understanding that they trade around these values

61
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:49,300
because sometimes as traders we expect paper to enter the market or commercial participants to enter the market every day.

62
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:55,200
Yet more often than not they are only there over the period of a week on the odd occasion.

63
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,800
Then, they influence market prices. Highly crucial to understand.

64
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:04,100
Commercial participants can move prices up or down.

65
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:10,700
So crucial to understand as they are probably the single most important participant that can move the market.

66
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:18,796
If they can influence prices up or down, we need to always be aware of when they enter the market and definitely not go against them.

67
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:25,500
Let's now continue with the second of our key market participants, the speculator.

68
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:31,428
The major difference between the speculator and the commercial participant is that

69
00:05:31,428 --> 00:05:38,399
the commercial participant tends to interact in the market and hold positions based on various fundamentals

70
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:43,100
or various technicals and they do tend to hold these toward maturity.

71
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:46,521
Whereas the speculator, they tend to

72
00:05:46,521 --> 00:05:48,842
hold until a profit target has

73
00:05:48,842 --> 00:05:53,098
been reached or an objective, a specific objective has been reached.

74
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:58,400
They speculate on the direction of price whether up or down and take advantage of that.

75
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:00,000
Once it has occurred, once it has occurred,

76
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:07,200
the advantage has been taken they then exit the position whereas commercial paper tends to hold on to the position.

77
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:11,900
So what are some of the characteristics that define speculative participants?

78
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:18,200
Well first and foremost speculative participants can be short or long term or even algorithmic.

79
00:06:18,800 --> 00:06:22,400
They tend to move price between longer term players.

80
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:26,600
In other words they tend to facilitate trade between the longer term participants.

81
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,700
They bring the longer term participants into the market by moving price up and down.

82
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:37,600
Speculators tend to be price sensitive. What do we mean by this?

83
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:44,000
Well, when prices move up or down, speculative positions tend to be unwound or put back on.

84
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:52,195
Because they are price sensitive in nature, they can sometimes also move the market quite aggressively in one direction or the other.

85
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:59,400
We will discuss various strategies based on speculative positioning later on in the course.

86
00:07:01,624 --> 00:07:04,024
Speculators tend to position with risk in mind.

87
00:07:04,324 --> 00:07:11,420
So they tend to enter at one point, but they do have a point where they exit the position or take the opposite side of the position to get out.

88
00:07:11,724 --> 00:07:13,438
Again, important to understand

89
00:07:13,438 --> 00:07:16,866
as sometimes it's speculators that are moving prices

90
00:07:16,866 --> 00:07:20,723
up and down as opposed to paper commercial participants.

91
00:07:22,424 --> 00:07:27,524
Speculators tend to take advantage or trade short-term anomalies within the market.

92
00:07:27,724 --> 00:07:34,024
Again, once that anomaly has been taken out of the market, they tend to exit those positions.

93
00:07:34,824 --> 00:07:38,524
And speculators tend to be non-influential on price.

94
00:07:38,524 --> 00:07:45,324
So they can move price, but not in the same aggressive manner that commercial paper can move the price.

95
00:07:45,624 --> 00:07:46,866
Again, important to understand

96
00:07:46,866 --> 00:07:49,351
because sometimes what happens is speculators of paper

97
00:07:49,351 --> 00:07:51,215
might move the market too far

98
00:07:51,215 --> 00:07:54,322
in one direction as a result of stops going off.

99
00:07:54,524 --> 00:07:59,624
And once those stops are taken out, we do tend to then see a rapid rebound in prices.

100
00:07:59,824 --> 00:08:05,324
So again, important to understand as we will discuss strategies based on this at a later point.

101
00:08:06,424 --> 00:08:12,424
Now, let's talk about the third key participant, the automated algorithmic strategy, or algos.

102
00:08:13,624 --> 00:08:17,824
Again, important to understand the key characteristics that make up these algorithms.

103
00:08:18,224 --> 00:08:20,328
Note, algorithms by their very

104
00:08:20,328 --> 00:08:23,273
nature are very short term and they

105
00:08:23,273 --> 00:08:27,902
tend to make up roughly 90% of the trade in most markets.

106
00:08:28,624 --> 00:08:29,824
Now it's key to understand this.

107
00:08:29,824 --> 00:08:32,321
If 90% of the trade is made up

108
00:08:32,321 --> 00:08:35,175
with short-term algorithms, we need to focus quite

109
00:08:35,175 --> 00:08:38,029
heavily on what their objectives and goals are

110
00:08:38,029 --> 00:08:42,666
of algorithms because we are going to be participating predominantly with these algorithms.

111
00:08:43,924 --> 00:08:50,924
So, another characteristic, their execution methodology is precise with exact entry and exit parameters.

112
00:08:51,224 --> 00:08:56,524
What this means is that they very rarely make mistakes, they very rarely hold on to losing positions.

113
00:08:57,024 --> 00:09:04,520
Yes, sometimes it does occur that they do, such as the flash crash, however, in general they are very precise with their execution methodology.

114
00:09:06,224 --> 00:09:13,618
Algorithms tend to speculate using high frequency, so they do a lot of trades very rapidly in and out, in and out.

115
00:09:13,924 --> 00:09:17,424
They tend to have very small downside with open-ended upside.

116
00:09:17,424 --> 00:09:19,330
So again, very often we

117
00:09:19,330 --> 00:09:21,998
can see markets sometimes moving very quickly

118
00:09:21,998 --> 00:09:24,285
in one direction and then reversing

119
00:09:24,285 --> 00:09:29,622
the trade, and this is more just noise or positioning unwinding happening within algorithms.

120
00:09:31,124 --> 00:09:35,024
Algorithms look to fill orders at best price practice within parameters.

121
00:09:35,124 --> 00:09:36,795
So again, algos are after

122
00:09:36,795 --> 00:09:39,134
the best possible price and because they

123
00:09:39,134 --> 00:09:41,139
can rapidly execute these orders a

124
00:09:41,139 --> 00:09:47,823
lot quicker than us, we need to understand their methodology in order to assist us in developing our own edge.

125
00:09:50,108 --> 00:09:54,308
Algorithms tend to facilitate trade, which is the same objective as the day trader.

126
00:09:54,708 --> 00:10:02,208
So important we understand this because it means that as a day trader, our edge is not necessarily disappears, but

127
00:10:02,208 --> 00:10:09,708
it's eroded by an algorithm or computer that can execute far better and far more efficiently than what we can.

128
00:10:09,908 --> 00:10:13,508
So again, important to understand this as we develop our edge as a trader.

129
00:10:14,108 --> 00:10:19,408
So, the key when it comes to trading market participants, we need to be able to answer the following.

130
00:10:19,808 --> 00:10:27,108
What are the goals, objectives, execution styles, time horizons of the various participants?

131
00:10:27,608 --> 00:10:30,690
If we can answer these four dynamics and

132
00:10:30,690 --> 00:10:33,772
understand the characteristics of each participant, we can

133
00:10:33,772 --> 00:10:36,083
develop some incredibly good strategies just

134
00:10:36,083 --> 00:10:40,706
knowing how each participant is interacting and where and when they interact.

135
00:10:42,872 --> 00:10:45,150
Now that we've spoken about the various key

136
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:47,143
participants in the market, let's talk a

137
00:10:47,143 --> 00:10:52,270
little bit more briefly about the various time frame participants that you can find in the financial markets.

138
00:10:53,172 --> 00:10:59,072
The most important thing to understand about time frame participants is change. What do we mean by change?

139
00:10:59,872 --> 00:11:06,072
Change influences market participants of every type, from the day trader to the long-term portfolio manager.

140
00:11:07,172 --> 00:11:11,272
Very simply put, change is merely prices moving up and down.

141
00:11:11,872 --> 00:11:17,272
This movement brings into the marketplace various different time frame participants.

142
00:11:17,772 --> 00:11:19,172
Let's use a basic example.

143
00:11:19,872 --> 00:11:23,072
Say now we took out the previous extreme of a previous day.

144
00:11:24,072 --> 00:11:26,662
The minute we take out that extreme, we're

145
00:11:26,662 --> 00:11:30,871
going to see other time frame participants reacting or responding to that change.

146
00:11:31,472 --> 00:11:35,272
It's that reaction that will dictate the future of price.

147
00:11:35,872 --> 00:11:42,469
And it's that reaction we need to understand and interpret in order to take advantage of the future movement in price.

148
00:11:43,272 --> 00:11:48,872
So let's talk a little bit more about some of these time frame participants that you can expect to see.

149
00:11:49,872 --> 00:11:52,872
The first and the shortest term time frame is the scalper.

150
00:11:53,772 --> 00:11:55,472
The scalper lives by the minute hand.

151
00:11:55,872 --> 00:12:00,572
It continually buys and sells in order to take advantage of discrepancies within the order flow.

152
00:12:01,272 --> 00:12:04,972
They are a master of reading order flow and rely solely on order flow.

153
00:12:06,572 --> 00:12:12,972
In essence, the scalper tends to be algorithmic in nature, however you'd do get human scalpers.

154
00:12:13,672 --> 00:12:20,472
They tend to execute large amount of orders, small winners and small losers and they are exact in their execution.

155
00:12:20,972 --> 00:12:25,572
Let's now take a look at a chart whereby we would be able to see scalpers interacting.

156
00:12:26,372 --> 00:12:31,772
As you can see on the left we have a basic profile, on the right we have a one minute chart.

157
00:12:32,372 --> 00:12:38,467
Now note the reason we have the one minute chart is because this is the time frame that scalpers tend to react on.

158
00:12:39,072 --> 00:12:41,905
Every little bit of movement up and

159
00:12:41,905 --> 00:12:44,738
down the page is a scalper interacting

160
00:12:44,738 --> 00:12:47,570
continuously as prices move up and down.

161
00:12:48,172 --> 00:12:53,772
Now as we can see in the example provided, we spent an entire day within the previous day's range.

162
00:12:54,272 --> 00:12:59,972
Most likely there was very little participation from other time frame participants.

163
00:13:00,372 --> 00:13:03,472
Most of the participation would have likely been the scalper.

164
00:13:04,172 --> 00:13:08,672
Understanding this will allow us to interpret what could occur on the next day.

165
00:13:11,768 --> 00:13:15,768
Let's talk about the next of our time frame participants, the day trader.

166
00:13:16,668 --> 00:13:19,268
The day trader tends to enter the market with no position.

167
00:13:19,968 --> 00:13:24,868
More importantly, the day trader leaves the market without a position. It has zero risk overnight.

168
00:13:25,668 --> 00:13:30,768
Day traders tend to use technical analysis and real order flow in order to make trading decisions.

169
00:13:31,568 --> 00:13:34,868
They depend on market generated information.

170
00:13:35,368 --> 00:13:37,643
Fundamentals tend to be slightly counterintuitive

171
00:13:37,643 --> 00:13:39,918
to the day trading participant as

172
00:13:39,918 --> 00:13:42,193
they tend to hinder the day

173
00:13:42,193 --> 00:13:44,468
trader as opposed to adding value.

174
00:13:45,268 --> 00:13:49,268
Let's take a look at the same example used in the scalping in the previous slide.

175
00:13:49,868 --> 00:13:52,068
As we can see, we have an inside day.

176
00:13:52,868 --> 00:13:58,068
Traditionally, inside day breaks, day traders tend to play or go with the break.

177
00:13:58,768 --> 00:14:05,865
As we can see, day traders will enter the market or will enter the market once we take out the previous day's high.

178
00:14:06,868 --> 00:14:12,168
They will be looking to hold for a continuation type pattern to the upside.

179
00:14:12,468 --> 00:14:14,007
However, do note day

180
00:14:14,007 --> 00:14:17,085
traders manage risk very effectively and any attempt

181
00:14:17,085 --> 00:14:20,163
at the market to re-enter the previous day's

182
00:14:20,163 --> 00:14:25,166
range, day traders will tend to exit those positions and zero their risk.

183
00:14:25,468 --> 00:14:27,668
This is a typical play from a day trader.

184
00:14:30,744 --> 00:14:35,544
The next time frame participant we're going to talk about is the short term traders.

185
00:14:36,444 --> 00:14:42,544
Short term traders tend to hold trades for longer than a day, sometimes even up to 3 to 5 days long.

186
00:14:43,944 --> 00:14:48,144
Short term traders give more attention to the technicals and fundamental factors.

187
00:14:48,944 --> 00:14:56,444
Just like the day trader who focuses on technicals, the short-term trader includes fundamental factors into their trading strategies.

188
00:14:57,444 --> 00:15:04,544
Short-term traders have a tendency to buy low and sell high looking for momentum breaks and momentum continuation patterns.

189
00:15:05,444 --> 00:15:10,644
Short-term traders accept and interpret fundamentals whilst using market-generated information.

190
00:15:11,344 --> 00:15:13,544
The key major difference between a day trader

191
00:15:13,544 --> 00:15:16,019
and a short term trader as we can see

192
00:15:16,019 --> 00:15:18,219
in the example provided is that as we

193
00:15:18,219 --> 00:15:23,444
said in the previous slide a day trader will look to play for the breakout of the inside day.

194
00:15:24,044 --> 00:15:31,540
Once or if that re-enters the previous day's range the day trader will have a tendency to exit that trade and flatten its risk.

195
00:15:32,144 --> 00:15:35,123
A swing or short term trader however would be

196
00:15:35,123 --> 00:15:38,102
more willing to hold on to that trade with

197
00:15:38,102 --> 00:15:41,743
a slightly bigger risk in the hope of slightly bigger profits.

198
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:50,060
The next time frame participant we're going to discuss is the intermediate trader or investor.

199
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:53,075
Intermediate traders or investors, also known

200
00:15:53,075 --> 00:15:56,028
as swing traders, they're very similar to short

201
00:15:56,028 --> 00:16:00,458
term traders but they operate from a slightly longer term time frame.

202
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:03,132
For instance, a short term trader would tend

203
00:16:03,132 --> 00:16:08,765
to operate from say a 30 minute to a 60 minute to even a daily candle or a daily time frame.

204
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:17,860
An intermediate trader or investor would tend to operate from a 60 minute, 4 hour, daily, weekly and monthly type time frame.

205
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:25,660
The intermediate trader finds a strong balance between fundamentals and market generated information.

206
00:16:25,960 --> 00:16:29,014
So they are equally balanced between technicals and

207
00:16:29,014 --> 00:16:34,358
fundamentals, whereas the day trader leans slightly more towards the technicals than the fundamentals.

208
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:38,598
As an example, if we look at the

209
00:16:38,598 --> 00:16:41,431
profile below, as we can see, we

